course: dictionary
lesson: 5587
title: Libel: untrue words that hurt
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A libel is a false statement, written or printed, that damages a person's good name. It has two key parts: the words are untrue, and they make people think badly of someone. «To quiet naughty babies they said the terrible grey Badger would up and get them» was a story told to frighten children, not a true fact. The writer calls it «a base libel on Badger», which means a mean, untrue thing said about him.
explain: How can you tell it is false? Read the clue after the dash: Badger «was rather fond of children». If he loved children, the story about him snatching them cannot be true. Always check a statement against what is really true about the person.
explain: Libel is about writing and printing. A newspaper that prints a false story that harms someone's reputation can be sued for libel. The spoken version of the same harm is called slander. Both are about honesty: saying untrue, harmful things about someone is wrong, and it can be against the law.
fact: Libel is a false written or published statement that damages a person's good name.
fact: To libel someone, as a verb, means to publish something false that harms their reputation.
fact: Libel is written or printed, for example in books, newspapers, online or on TV. Slander is spoken.
fact: «A base libel» means a mean, untrue thing said about someone.
fact: In UK spelling you write libelled and libelling; in US spelling you write libeled and libeling.
exercise: «To quiet naughty babies they said the terrible grey Badger would up and get them.» Was this story true? | It was a fact from a science book | Yes, every word was true | *No, it was a false story, a libel on Badger | It was a poem about the seaside
exercise: What does libel mean? | A kind poem written for a friend | A loud shout across a field | *A false written statement that harms someone's good name | A true report of the day's weather
exercise: Which word means the spoken kind of untrue, harmful talk? | Libel | *Slander | Fact | Poem
test: A newspaper prints a made-up story that ruins a famous singer's good name. What is this? | *Libel | Fact | Poem | Compliment
test: Why is libel wrong? | *It spreads untrue, harmful things, which goes against honesty | It makes people laugh | It only hurts the person who says it | It is a kind thing to say about people
exam: Which one is a libel? | Writing a poem about the sea | Saying a teacher is kind when she really is kind | Telling your friend the time | *Printing in a newspaper that a teacher steals, when it is not true
